+### Compound Durations
+
+`parseCompound` sums several pieces into one total in milliseconds. Pieces can be separated by spaces, commas, or nothing at all.
+
+```ts
+import { parseCompound } from 'ms';
+
+parseCompound('1h 30m');         // 5400000
+parseCompound('1h, 30m');        // 5400000
+parseCompound('2 days 4 hours'); // 187200000
+```
+
+Each piece is parsed like `parse`, so decimals and negatives work (`'1.5h -30m'`). When there is more than one piece, every piece needs a unit (`'1h 30'` throws). Invalid input throws a `TypeError`.
+
+### Clock Strings
+
+`parseClock` parses timer-style strings: `m:ss` and `h:mm:ss`. It returns milliseconds.
+
+```ts
+import { parseClock } from 'ms';
+
+parseClock('1:30'); // 90000
+parseClock('01:02:03'); // 3723000
+```
+
+Seconds must be two digits and below 60 (and so must minutes in `h:mm:ss`). Invalid input throws a `TypeError`.
+
 ### Enhanced Type Safety
 
 If you want strict type checking for the input value, you can use `parseStrict`.
